{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788381735889,"sessionID":"ses_f9c24d349ffeKHnaVF2lrb6ncE","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_06088632b1864086a170f0ca","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"ls -R runtime"},"output":"runtime:\ntoylang.c\n","metadata":{"output":"runtime:\ntoylang.c\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"ls -R runtime","time":{"start":1788381735886,"end":1788381735888}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now I understand the structure. Let me look at how the JS backend handles Float in the places where Lua currently has `unreachable!(\"Float is JS-only in this row\")`:\n\n1. `show` function (printer) — line 458-459: `Type::Int | Type::Int64 | Type::Bool => format!(\"tostring({value})\")` and `Type::Float => unreachable!`. For JS it's `Type::Int | Type::Int64 | Type::Float | Type::Bool => format!(\"String({value})\")`.\n\nWait, the JS one is: `Type::Int | Type::Int64 | Type::Float | Type::Bool => format!(\"String({value})\")`. For Lua, `tostring` on a float gives what? Lua's `tostring` on a float uses Lua's own number formatting (%.14g by default in PUC-Rio Lua 5.4? Actually Lua 5.3+ uses `%.14g`). That may not agree with the JS shortest round-trip. Hmm.\n\nThe issue says:\n\"Not yet settled, needs a call during implementation: exact float literal printing so all seven backends agree byte for byte (default float formatting differs across languages) -- pick a format and hold every backend to it.\"\n\nSo the float printing format is not settled. The ADR 0007 says float is JavaScript's double. In JS, `String(number)` produces the shortest round-trip decimal (the ECMAScript Number::toString algorithm). For Lua, `tostring(0.1)` gives \"0.1\" in modern Lua (5.3+, which uses `%.14g`, and 0.1 prints as \"0.1\"). Hmm, but there could be differences: JS `String(1e21)` gives \"1e+21\"; Lua's `tostring(1e21)` gives \"1e+21\" too? Actually Lua 5.4 `tostring(1e21)` produces \"1e+21\". But `String(100000000000000000000)` in JS gives \"100000000000000000000\"? No. JS: `String(1e21)` → \"1e+21\". Actually ECMAScript specifies for numbers >= 1e21, use exponent notation with \"+\". Lua's `%.14g` uses C's %g which uses exponent when exponent < -4 or >= precision. With precision 14, 1e21 exponent is 21 >= 14, so \"1e+21\". Hmm so they may agree in many cases but not exactly: JS uses shortest round-trip (which can be more precise than 14 significant digits in some cases where 14 is not enough to round-trip). E.g., 0.1 + 0.2 = 0.30000000000000004. JS String gives \"0.30000000000000004\". Lua `tostring` gives? In Lua 5.3+ (with `%.14g`), 0.30000000000000004 prints as \"0.30000000000000004\"? Wait 0.30000000000000004 has 17 significant digits. `%.14g` would round it to \"0.3\". Let me think. Actually Lua 5.3 changed to `%.14g` from `%.14g`... Hmm. Lua 5.3 release notes: \"The default format for the numbers is %.14g\". Let me verify: `tostring(0.30000000000000004)` in Lua 5.4 → \"0.3\"? Since 0.3 as %.14g → \"0.3\". Yes, that would disagree with JS's \"0.30000000000000004\".\n\nSo for byte-for-byte agreement, Lua can't just use `tostring`. It needs a host-hook (the task says \"using Lua host-hook syntax\"). \n\n\"using Lua host-hook syntax\" — this means using the Lua host functions (e.g., `tl_*` helpers injected by the runtime). The runtime `run_lua` presumably provides helpers. Let me look at the runtime directory.\n\nThe Lua backend runs through a host that injects helpers. Let me check runtime/.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_063dbabc1001GaVkE4fA1jonQO","sessionID":"ses_f9c24d349ffeKHnaVF2lrb6ncE","messageID":"msg_063db884a001s6ukrD8o46atFb"}}
