{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788381784989,"sessionID":"ses_f9c24d349ffeKHnaVF2lrb6ncE","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_cef6f97c92d145aa804f1b73","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-lua/Cargo.toml"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-lua/Cargo.toml</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: [package]\n2: name = \"toylang\"\n3: version = \"0.1.0\"\n4: edition = \"2024\"\n5: \n6: [dependencies]\n7: anyhow = \"1.0.104\"\n8: inkwell = { version = \"0.10.0\", features = [\"llvm22-1\"] }\n9: mlua = { version = \"0.12.0\", features = [\"lua54\", \"vendored\", \"anyhow\"] }\n10: serde_json = \"1.0.151\"\n11: tempfile = \"3.27.0\"\n12: winnow = \"1.0.4\"\n13: \n14: [dev-dependencies]\n15: insta = \"1.48.0\"\n16: serde = { version = \"1.0.229\", features = [\"derive\"] }\n17: serde_norway = \"0.9.42\"\n18: \n19: # build.rs reuses the parser (winnow) to check prelude.toy at build time; see build.rs itself.\n20: [build-dependencies]\n21: winnow = \"1.0.4\"\n22: \n23: # The three lints the quality hooks forward; thresholds live in clippy.toml.\n24: # On in the manifest so plain `cargo clippy` sees them: the Stop hook adds\n25: # nothing of its own. See plans/quality-practices.md.\n26: [lints.clippy]\n27: cognitive_complexity = \"warn\"\n28: too_many_lines = \"warn\"\n29: fn_params_excessive_bools = \"warn\"\n\n(End of file - total 29 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"[package]\nname = \"toylang\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\nedition = \"2024\"\n\n[dependencies]\nanyhow = \"1.0.104\"\ninkwell = { version = \"0.10.0\", features = [\"llvm22-1\"] }\nmlua = { version = \"0.12.0\", features = [\"lua54\", \"vendored\", \"anyhow\"] }\nserde_json = \"1.0.151\"\ntempfile = \"3.27.0\"\nwinnow = \"1.0.4\"\n\n[dev-dependencies]\ninsta = \"1.48.0\"\nserde = { version = \"1.0.229\", features = [\"derive\"] }\nserde_norway = \"0.9.42\"\n\n# build.rs reuses the parser (winnow) to check prelude.toy at build time; see build.rs itself.\n[build-dependencies]","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-lua/Cargo.toml","text":"[package]\nname = \"toylang\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\nedition = \"2024\"\n\n[dependencies]\nanyhow = \"1.0.104\"\ninkwell = { version = \"0.10.0\", features = [\"llvm22-1\"] }\nmlua = { version = \"0.12.0\", features = [\"lua54\", \"vendored\", \"anyhow\"] }\nserde_json = \"1.0.151\"\ntempfile = \"3.27.0\"\nwinnow = \"1.0.4\"\n\n[dev-dependencies]\ninsta = \"1.48.0\"\nserde = { version = \"1.0.229\", features = [\"derive\"] }\nserde_norway = \"0.9.42\"\n\n# build.rs reuses the parser (winnow) to check prelude.toy at build time; see build.rs itself.\n[build-dependencies]\nwinnow = \"1.0.4\"\n\n# The three lints the quality hooks forward; thresholds live in clippy.toml.\n# On in the manifest so plain `cargo clippy` sees them: the Stop hook adds\n# nothing of its own. See plans/quality-practices.md.\n[lints.clippy]\ncognitive_complexity = \"warn\"\ntoo_many_lines = \"warn\"\nfn_params_excessive_bools = \"warn\"","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":29,"totalLines":29,"truncated":false}},"title":"Cargo.toml","time":{"start":1788381784968,"end":1788381784987}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now I understand how the Lua backend runs: it's embedded via mlua, and the emitted Lua source uses `tl_*` helper functions defined in source via the `*_HELPER` constants, except `print` which is injected as a host function. There's also the `tl_none` mentioned in a comment (`falls through to `tl_none`, the absent Opt.`) but I don't see a `tl_none` constant... let me check. Actually in the Match arm comment line 981-982: \"a partial chain tests every arm and falls through to `tl_none`, the absent Opt.\" But the code writes `return \"none\" ` directly. OK, comment drift, not my problem.\n\nNow the task says \"port emit_js.rs Float handling into src/emit_lua.rs using Lua host-hook syntax\". \n\nWhat does \"Lua host-hook syntax\" mean? I think it means the emitted Lua source should call a `tl_*` helper function (host-hook) for float printing, since Lua's native `tostring` doesn't produce the shortest round-trip representation matching JS. Like the JS backend uses `String({value})` — which produces the shortest round-trip — the Lua backend needs its own way to produce the same bytes. The task mentions \"Lua host-hook syntax\" — maybe meaning the emission should call a helper like `tl_fmt` or similar, hosted by the runtime (`run_lua` in Rust, like `print` and `tl_next_input` are host functions).\n\nWait, \"host-hook\" — in this repo, the `tl_*` helper functions defined in the emitted source are \"helpers\". But the ones injected by the Rust host are \"host functions\" (like `print`, `tl_next_input`). Actually the comments call them helpers: the constants `SELECT_HELPER` etc. are emitted Lua source functions. The `run_lua` host injects `print` and `tl_next_input`.\n\nHmm. \"Lua host-hook syntax\" — I think this refers to using a `tl_`-prefixed hook function. So we need to emit something like `tl_show_float(x)` which the host provides (or a helper emitted in source that formats a float as JS's shortest round-trip). \n\nBut wait — what is the agreed float printing format? The issue says:\n\n\"Not yet settled, needs a call during implementation: exact float literal printing so all seven backends agree byte for byte (default float formatting differs across languages) -- pick a format and hold every backend to it.\"\n\nSo it's not settled. But the reference implementation (emit_js.rs) uses JS's `String()` which produces the ECMAScript Number::toString shortest round-trip. And `float::lit` uses Rust's `f64::to_string()` which is also the shortest round-trip decimal (Rust's Display for f64 is the shortest representation that round-trips, per Rust docs). And the JS `String(number)` produces the same shortest round-trip. So the two agree.\n\nNow the acceptance criteria say \"NaN/Infinity are producible and print identically across backends\". JS `String(Infinity)` → \"Infinity\", `String(NaN)` → \"NaN\". Rust's `f64::to_string()` for infinity gives \"inf\" and NaN gives \"NaN\" — wait. Rust's `Display for f64` prints \"inf\" for infinity and \"-inf\" for negative infinity, and \"NaN\" for NaN. So the JS backend prints via `String(value)` at runtime: for a Float infinity value, JS prints \"Infinity\". For Rust's `f64::to_string()`, it gives \"inf\". Different!\n\nBut wait — for the JS backend, the Float literal printing at compile-time uses `float::lit`, which panics on non-finite. But at runtime, arithmetic produces infinity, and the printer `show` prints via `String(value)` at runtime, which gives \"Infinity\" for JS. So each backend's runtime formatting determines the printed output.\n\nFor Lua, at runtime a float infinity is `math.huge`, and `tostring(math.huge)` gives \"inf\" in Lua. That differs from JS's \"Infinity\".\n\nSo we need a host-hook to format floats the JS way: \"Infinity\", \"-Infinity\", \"NaN\", and for finite values the shortest round-trip decimal.\n\nHow to produce the shortest round-trip decimal in Rust? Rust's `f64::to_string()` gives shortest round-trip but with \"inf\"/\"NaN\" spelling. JS's format: NaN→\"NaN\", ∞→\"Infinity\", -∞→\"-Infinity\". Finite: JS uses the shortest round-trip, e.g. `String(0.1)` →\"0.1\", `String(1e21)` →\"1e+21\", `String(0.000001)` →\"0.000001\"? Let me recall ECMAScript Number::toString. For finite numbers, JS produces either decimal notation or exponent notation, choosing the one with fewer characters, with specific rules: exponent notation used when exponent < -6 or >= 21 (for toString with radix 10). Actually: the spec says: if k <= n <= 21, use decimal; if 0 < n <= 21 use decimal with k-n zeros; if -6 < n <= 0, use decimal with n leading zeros; otherwise use exponent notation \"xey\" with exponent sign.\n\nRust's `f64::to_string()` uses a similar shortest round-trip algorithm but slightly different thresholds? Let me think. Rust's Display for f64: `0.0000001` (1e-7) prints as \"0.0000001\"? Rust's shortest repr uses Grisu, and prints `1e-7`? Hmm. Let me actually test.\n\nI need to verify what Rust's `f64::to_string()` produces for various values and compare with JS. Let me test with a quick Rust program... but wait, I shouldn't use /tmp writes? The denial says writes under /tmp are denied, but the bash tool writes are denied... Actually the denial list says \"writes under /tmp\" — probably for bash commands. I can use the write tool to create files in the repo but not scratch... Hmm.\n\n. Actually the denials listed are for bash commands. But I can run `cargo` commands. I can run a quick check using the existing test infra, or I could run `cargo test` with a temporary test... \n\nActually, a simpler approach: since the JS backend already exists and is the reference, and `float::lit` is already the literal spellings used by all backends (the comment says \"Every backend emits a literal through here\"), the question is only about the runtime printer (the `show` function) for Float values produced by arithmetic. \n\nFor Lua, we need a host-hook `tl_fmt` that produces JS-identical output. Since the Lua backend is embedded in Rust (mlua), we can implement the hook in Rust using... what? We need shortest round-trip decimal + JS spelling of non-finite. \n\nThe simplest robust approach: implement the host hook `tl_show_float(x)` using Rust: format the float using the same algorithm JS uses. But `f64::to_string()` gives \"inf\"/\"NaN\"/\"0.30000000000000004\"? Let me recall what Rust gives for 0.1+0.2 = 0.30000000000000004: Rust's `format!(\"{}\", 0.1_f64 + 0.2_f64)` → \"0.30000000000000004\". Yes, shortest round-trip.\n\nWhat does Rust give for `1e21`? Rust `format!(\"{}\", 1e21_f64)` → \"1000000000000000000000\"? Hmm. Actually I recall Rust's Display for f64 gives \"1000000000000000000000\" for 1e21? Or \"1e21\"? Let me think. Rust's float Display uses shortest repr via Grisu-Dragon, and the formatting decisions: I believe Rust prints `1e21` as \"1000000000000000000000\". Hmm. Actually no. Let me recall: In Rust, `println!(\"{}\", 1e21_f64)` prints \"1000000000000000000000\". I genuinely am not sure.\n\n.\n\nJS `String(1e21)` → \"1e+21\". If Rust gives \"1000000000000000000000\", they'd disagree. So we can't just use Rust's Display for the hook either — the corpus harness compares across backends byte for byte, and the JS reference output would be \"1e+21\".\n\nHmm. So what's the \"format\" decision? The issue says \"pick a format and hold every backend to it.\" So this lane (Lua) needs to pick a format for float printing, consistent with JS's. Since JS already emits via `String()`, the de facto reference format is ECMAScript's Number::toString. So for Lua, the hook must replicate ECMAScript's algorithm exactly to produce byte-identical output.\n\n.\n\n\n\nLet me verify ECMAScript Number::toString algorithm precisely. From ECMA-262 spec (Number::toString, and the `ToString` abstract operation):\n\nGiven x (a Number), produce decimal representation:\n- If x is NaN, return \"NaN\".\n- If x is +0 or -0, return \"0\" (and for -0, also \"0\").\n- If x < 0, return \"-\" + ToString(-x).\n- If x is +∞, return \"Infinity\"; if -∞, \"-Infinity\".\n- Otherwise, let n, k, s be integers such that k >= 1, 10^(k-1) <= s < 10^k, s×10^(n-k) = x, k is as small as possible. (I.e., s is the shortest decimal digits of x.)\n  If k <= n <= 21: return digits s with n-k zeros appended.\n. Wait let me recall the exact spec.\n\nECMA-262 §6.1.6.1.20 \"Number::toString ( x )\":\n\n1. If x is NaN, return the String \"NaN\".\n2. If x is either +0𝔽 or -0𝔽, return the String \"0\".\n3. If x < +0𝔽, return the StringConcatenation of \"-\" and ! Number::toString(-x).\n4. If x is +∞𝔽, return the String \"Infinity\".\n5. Otherwise, let n, k, and s be integers such that k ≥ 1, 10***(k - 1) ≤ s < 10**k, s × 10***(n - k) = x, and k is as small as possible. Note that k is the number of digits in the decimal representation of s, that s is not divisible by 10, and that the least significant digit of s is not necessarily uniquely determined by these criteria.\n6. If k ≤ n ≤ 21, return the StringConsisting of the code units of the digits of the decimal representation of s (in order,, followed by n - k occurrences of the code unit 0x0030 (DIGIT ZERO).\n7. If 0 < n ≤ 21, return the StringConsisting of the code units of the digits of the decimal representation of s, followed by n-k occurrences of the code unit 0x0030 (DIGIT ZERO).\n8. If -6 < n ≤ 0, return the StringConsisting of the code unit 0x0030 (DIGIT ZERO,, followed by the code unit 0x002E (FULL STOP,, followed by -n occurrences of the code unit 0x0030 (DIGIT ZERO,, followed by the code units of the digits of the decimal representation of s.\n9. Otherwise, if k == 1, return the StringConsisting of the code units of the digits of the decimal representation of s, followed by the code unit 0x0065 (LATIN SMALL LETTER E,, followed by the code unit 0x002B (PLUS SIGN,, or the code unit 0x002D (HYPHEN-MINUS,, followed by the code unit of the decimal representation of the abs (n - 1.\n10. Return the StringConsisting of the code units of the digits of the decimal representation of s, followed by the code unit 0x0065 (LATIN SMALL LETTER E,, followed by the code unit 0x002B (PLUS SIGN,, or the code unit 0x002D (HYPHEN-MINUS,, followed by the code unit of the decimal representation of the abs (n - 1,, followed by the code unit 0x0030 (DIGIT ZERO,, followed by the code units of the decimal representation of n - 1.\n\nWait, step  ‎10 — that's wrong. Let me recall the actual steps 9 and 10:\n\n9. Otherwise, if k ≤ n, return the StringConsisting of the code units of the digits of the decimal representation of s, followed by n-k occurrences of the code unit 0x0030 (DIGIT ZERO,, followed by the code unit 0x0065 (LATIN SMALL LETTER E,, followed by the code unit 0x002B (PLUS SIGN,, or the code unit 0x002D (HYPHEN-MINUS,, followed by the code unit of the decimal representation of the abs (n - 1.\n10. Otherwise, return the StringConsisting of the code units of the digits of the decimal representation of s, followed by the code unit 0x0065 (LATIN SMALL LETTER E,, followed by the code unit 0x002B (PLUS SIGN,, or the code unit 0x002D (HYPHEN-MINUS,, followed by the code unit of the decimal representation of the abs (n - 1,, followed by the code unit 0x0030 (DIGIT ZERO,, followed by the code units of the decimal representation of n - 1.,\n\nNo wait. The exponent part: for k > n (i.e., the decimal point sits before the digits), e.g., s = 123, n =  2?, hmm. Let me look up the actual algorithm more carefully. This is confusing. Let me just find the actual algorithm from MDN or the spec.\n\nActually, simpler: Node/V8's behavior (which the JS backend uses via `node`) is what we need to match. `String(0.1)` → \"0.1\"; `String(1e21)` → \"1e+21\"; `String(1e-7)` → \"1e-7\"; `String(0.000001)` (1e-6) → \"0.000001\". Because -6 < n <= 0 uses decimal. 1e-7 has n = -6? Let me compute: x = 1e-7 = 0.0000001. s×10^(n-k) = x with s=1, k=1. So 10^(n-1) = 1e-7 → n-1 = -7 → n = -6. Since -6 < n <= 0 fails (n = -6, condition is -6 < n), so we go to exponent case, giving \"1e-7\". And 0.000001 = 1e-6: n = -5, -6 < -5 <= 0 holds, so decimal: \"0.000001\". Good.\n\nFor 1e21: s=1,k=1, 10^(n-1)=1e21 → n=22. k<=n<=21? No (n=22). 0<n<=21? No. -6<n<=0? No. So exponent case: k==1, so \"1e+21\". Wait step  ‎9: \"Otherwise, if k == 1, return s + \"e\" + sign + abs(n-1)\". So \"1\" + \"e\" + \"+\" + \"21\" = \"1e+21\". \n\nFor 123456789012345680000 (n=21): k<=n<=21 → decimal with digits s (21 digits), followed by n-k zeros. s is the digits of 1.2345678901234568e20? Hmm.\n\nOK, the exact algorithm matters. Rather than reimplement it by hand (error-prone), I could implement it in the Rust host-hook using an existing crate. Is there a crate for \"shortest round-trip float to JS string\"? Options:\n- The `ryu` crate produces shortest round-trip decimal but in its own format (`ryu::Buffer::format` gives e.g. \"0.30000000000000004\", and \"1E21\" with uppercase E? Actually ryu's `format` gives \"1E21\" (uppercase E, no plus sign).. Not JS-compatible.\n.\n- `dtoa` crate? \n\nAlternatively, since the JS backend is the reference and already produces correct output via `node`, the host hook in Rust could shell out... no.\n\nWait — maybe the intended approach is simpler than I think. Let me reconsider \"Lua host-hook syntax\". Perhaps the intent is to add a `tl_show` host function in `run_lua` in Rust that calls... hmm, what would produce JS-identical bytes? \n\nIdea: In the Rust host, we could use `ryu` (already a dependency? Let me check Cargo.toml) and adjust. Or, better: we can implement the ECMAScript algorithm directly in Rust using Rust's own shortest-round-trip representation from `f64::to_string()` and then transform it into JS notation. Since Rust's `to_string()` already produces the shortest decimal digits `s` exactly (Grisu/Dragon4 shortes), we just need to convert that representation into the ECMAScript format (decimal vs exponential, exponent spelling \"e+21\" etc.».\n\nRust's `f64::to_string()` format: for a finite non-zero value, it produces either plain decimal or \"e\" notation. What does Rust produce for 1e21? Let me recall... I believe Rust's Display for f64 prints `1e21` as \"1e21\"? Hmm. Let me just test.\n\nActually, let me check what Rust's Display gives. I'll run a small cargo test or use the existing test harness. Actually simplest: run `cargo run -- fmt`? no. Let me use a quick rust eval via the repo's own toolchain: I can add a temporary test... that's messy. \n\nAlternatively I recall: Rust's `f64::to_string()` produces: \n- `(0.1_f64).to_string()` → \"0.1\"\n- `(1e21_f64).to_string()` → \"1000000000000000000000\"? Hmm. I think Rust's float formatting uses a \"shortest repr\" via the Grisu algorithm, and the formatting code in `core::fmt` for floats produces plain decimal unless... Actually I recall testing this: `println!(\"{}\", 1e21f64);` prints \"1000000000000000000000\". And `println!(\"{}\", 1e-7f64);` prints \"0.0000001\". Rust does NOT use exponent notation in its Display for floats! I'm fairly confident: Rust's `{}` for f64 never uses exponent notation — it always expands fully. E.g., `println!(\"{}\", 1e300)` → a 300-digit number. Yes, that's right — Rust's float Display expands everything (which is why `to_string` on huge/small floats gives giant strings).\n\nSo I can't rely on Rust's Display for the exponent style. \n\nHmm. So implementing the ECMAScript algorithm requires getting the shortest significant digits `s`, which Rust's `to_string` gives us (as a fully-expanded decimal string...). But then I'd have to re-derive s and n from the expanded string, and re-apply the EC format rules. That's doable: parse the expanded decimal string into digits + exponent, then apply EC formatting. But careful with rounding: Rust's shortest repr is the same shortest repr JS uses (both produce the shortest decimal that round-trips, and both pick... the same one? Not necessarily! For a given double there may be multiple shortest decimals that round-trip (e.g., both \"0.3\" and \"0.30000000000000004\" round-trip to the same double? No — 0.3 round-trips to the double nearest 0.3, while 0.30000000000000004 round-trips to a different double (0.1+0.2's result.). Hmm, but for some doubles, two different shortest-length decimals could round-trip. JS and Rust might pick different ones in theory. But in practice both use \"shortest, ties toward... the closest\"? JS's algorithm: among shortest, pick the closest to x; if tie, pick the even last digit? Actually JS: \"the decimal representation with the smallest number of digits; if there are multiple, the one closest to x; if tie, the one with even last digit\"? I recall the spec says: \"the shortest digit sequence s...  where s is not divisible by 10, and the least significant digit of s is not necessarily uniquely determined\" — implementation-defined among shortest? Hmm.\n\nThe ECMA spec leaves the choice among shortest representations open (\"the least significant digit is not necessarily uniquely determined by these criteria\"). But V8/JS engines choose the shortest one closest to the value. Rust's shortest repr also picks closest. So they should agree. Rust's `to_string()` and JS `String()` agree on the digit sequence for finite values in practice (both shortest round-trip closest)..\n\nBut wait — the corpus harness compares across backends byte-for-byte, and the JS backend's output IS the emitted node program's output via `String()`. So whatever JS gives, Lua must match. Given they both produce shortest-round-trip, they agree on finite values. Good.\n\n.\n\nSo the plan: implement a host hook `tl_show` in `run_lua` (or as an emitted helper?— hmm, \"host-hook syntax\") that formats a Lua number the ECMAScript way.\n\n.\n\nActually wait. Let me reconsider \"Lua host-hook syntax\" again. Maybe it means: the emitted Lua source uses a helper like `tl_float(x)` defined as a host-injected function (like `print`), rather than `tostring`. But the hooks (the `tl_*` helpers) are currently emitted as Lua source constants, not host-injected. The host-injected ones are `print` and `tl_next_input`. Hmm. The task explicitly says \"using Lua host-hook syntax\". I think this means: emit a call to a `tl_*` function (the host-hook convention in this file), i.e., add a `tl_show_float` or similar helper, defined in the emitted Lua source or injected by the Rust host. \n\nGiven the formatting needs Rust-level float manipulation (JS-format), the cleanest is to define the function as a Rust host hook injected into the Lua globals in `run_lua`, the way `print` and `tl_next_input` are. But the emitted source must call it, and the helper needs to be \"used\" tracking. Hmm — but the helpers in `emit_lua.rs` are emitted into the source. A host-injected function wouldn't be emitted; it'd be set in `run_lua` unconditionally or conditionally. \n\nBut wait — the `step_*.rs` tests snapshot the emitted Lua source. If we emit a call to `tl_show_float` but the helper is host-injected (not in source), the snapshot would show a call to an undefined-in-source function, but that's fine because at runtime the host defines it. Actually, hmm, `print` is exactly that: emitted as `print(...)`, and the host injects it. So \"host-hook syntax\" = calling a `tl_*` function that the host provides. \n\nAlternatively, we could emit the helper as Lua source, like `ARITH_HELPER`. But implementing ECMAScript float formatting in Lua source is complex and error-prone (Lua has no bit manipulation easily for doubles, though it does have `math.frexp`, `math.ldexp`...). Actually implementing shortest-round-trip in Lua is hard. Much better in Rust host.\n\n.\n\nHmm, wait. Actually, maybe there's an even simpler approach: since `run_lua` is embedded in Rust, the host hook can just produce the JS-format string using a small helper written in Rust. But what does \"JS-format\" require in terms of the shortest digits? We can use Rust's `f64::to_string()` to get shortest digits (fully expanded), then normalize to EC format. Let me write a Rust function:\n\n```\nfn js_number_string(x: f64) -> String {\n    if x.is_nan() { return \"NaN\".to_string(); }\n    if x.is_infinite() { return if x > 0.0 { \"Infinity\" } else { \"-Infinity\" }; }\n    if x == 0.0 { return \"0\"; }\n    let neg = x < 0.0;\n    let x = x.abs();\n    let s = x.to_string();  // fully expanded shortest repr, e.g. \"123.45\" or \"0.000001\" or \"1000000000000000000000\"\n    ...\n}\n```\n\nHmm, but there's a subtlety: Rust's `to_string()` for values like `1e-7` gives \"0.0000001\"? And the shortest digits are \"1\", n = -6. We need to re-derive s and n. \n\nApproach: use the string to extract digits and exponent:\n\nGiven the fully expanded string (e.g. \"123.45\", \"0.000001\", \"1000\", \"0.0000001\", \"30000000000000004\"? hmm 0.30000000000000004 prints as \"0.30000000000000004\"...), we can compute the digits s (stripped of decimal point and leading/trailing zeros...but careful: trailing zeros must be removed because s is not divisible by 10). Actually Rust's shortest repr: for 0.1, \"0.1\" → digits \"1\". For 100.0, Rust gives \"100\"? For `100.0_f64.to_string()` → \"100\". Digits \"1\", n=3. For 1e21 → Rust gives \"1000000000000000000000\" (22 digits..., trailing zeros), digits after stripping trailing zeros: \"1\", n=22. Good. For 123456789012345680000 (which is 1.2345678901234568e20), Rust gives \"123456789012345680000\"→ strip trailing zeros → \"12345678901234568\"? Wait, the value 1.2345678901234568e20 = 123456789012345680000, shortest digits sare \"12345678901234568\" (17 digits), n=21. Rust's string \"123456789012345680000\" — trailing zeros: strip → \"12345678901234568\", and the exponent n = number of digits before the decimal point = 21. Good.\n\nSo algorithm:\n1. Handle NaN, ±Infinity, ±0, sign.\n2. Let `s` = x.abs().to_string() — fully expanded shortest repr.\n.\n  For integers ≥ 1: no decimal point.\n. For values < 1: \"0.000...123\". \n3. Strip the decimal point: digits string, and count digits before point → n (the position of decimal point). Let me define n = exponent such that value = 0.d1d2... × 10^n? Hmm. Let me define: s×10^(n-k) = x, with k = number of digits in s. So n is: for x = 123.45, s=\"12345\", k=5, n=3 (10^(5-3))=10^2, 12345×10^-2 = 123.45? No. Wait: s×10^(n-k) = x → 12345 × 10^(n-5) = 123.45 → n-5 = -2 → n = 3. And the \"number of digits before the decimal point\" for 123.45 is 3. Yes, n = digits-before-point. Good.\n4. Then apply EC formatting rules using s and n.\n\nNow the tricky part: get the fully expanded string, but Rust's `to_string()` never uses exponents so it's always fully expanded? For very large values like 1e300, Rust's to_string gives a 301-character string of digits. OK. And for subnormals like 5e-324, gives \"0.000...0005\" with 323 zeros. OK, works but verbose. Fine.\n\nActually wait — is Rust's `to_string()` really fully expanded always? Let me recall: `(1e21_f64).to_string()` — I'm now doubting. Let me test this quickly. I can write a tiny Rust test in the repo... but better, I can just run a one-liner via `cargo script`? Not installed. \n\nLet me just check by writing a temporary test file in tests/ ... but that would pollute. Alternatively use `rustc` with an inline file — but that's writing a file, which I'd have to write with the write tool (allowed in the worktree? I'm told to create/edit files only with write/edit tools, and scratch files are fine as long as I don't git add them.). I could write /tmp/opencode/test.rs and run rustc. But \"writes under /tmp\" denied for bash — but the write tool might be allowed? The denial list says \"writes under /tmp\" — likely for bash commands. The brief says \"CREATE AND EDIT FILES ONLY WITH YOUR write/edit TOOLS\". So I can use the write tool to write /tmp/opencode/check.rs? But the brief says work ONLY inside this worktree. Hmm — writing a scratch file to /tmp might violate \"work ONLY inside this worktree\". But /tmp/opencode is pre-approved for temporary work per the tool description. However the task brief says \"Scratch files you cannot delete are FINE\". And \"writes under /tmp\" are in the KNOWN DENIALS list (presumably for bash redirection).. \n\nSimplest safe route: I'll test Rust's float Display behavior via an existing test — e.g., add a tiny temporary #[test] in the worktree? That also pollutes. \n\nActually, I realize I can check Rust's float formatting via the already-available `cargo` by running a test that already exists... no. \n\nAlternatively, I can just trust my knowledge and also make the host-hook robust: instead of relying on Rust's Display, use `ryu` if available, or implement digit extraction from Rust's Display. Let me check Cargo.toml for existing deps (ryu? dtoa? serde_json already there.).","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_063dc6b7a001Z8b2oq2sLE8u1B","sessionID":"ses_f9c24d349ffeKHnaVF2lrb6ncE","messageID":"msg_063dbb7f80010EOEQwozxin46M"}}
