{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788381796478,"sessionID":"ses_f9c24d349ffeKHnaVF2lrb6ncE","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_395525696ec742bcbdaa46c8","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"grep -n \"tl_sum\\|tl_div\\|Sum\\|arith\" src/emit_js.rs | head -40"},"output":"65:function tl_div(a, b) {\n78:const ARITH64_HELPER: &str = r#\"function tl_div64(a, b) {\n173:function tl_sum(v) {\n178:function tl_sum64(v) {\n211:        (used.arith, ARITH_HELPER),\n212:        (used.arith64, ARITH64_HELPER),\n505:    arith: bool,\n506:    arith64: bool,\n617:                used.sum |= *which == Builtin::Sum && tir::runtime_elem(&arg.ty) == Some(&Type::Int);\n619:                    *which == Builtin::Sum && tir::runtime_elem(&arg.ty) == Some(&Type::Int64);\n625:                    used.arith64 = true;\n627:                    used.arith = true;\n815:        Kind::Arith { op, lhs, rhs } => arith(&t.ty, *op, expr(enums, lhs), expr(enums, rhs)),\n871:            Builtin::Sum => {\n873:                    format!(\"tl_sum({})\", expr(enums, arg))\n875:                    format!(\"tl_sum64({})\", expr(enums, arg))\n1052:/// One arithmetic expression at the width the node's type names. BigInt operators are exact\n1055:fn arith(ty: &Type, op: BinOp, l: String, r: String) -> String {\n1058:        // arithmetic, with no wrap to spell. Division by zero is the IEEE answer, Infinity,\n1059:        // which is why there is no `tl_div` guard here unlike the integer widths.\n1065:            other => unreachable!(\"{other} is not arithmetic\"),\n1069:            BinOp::Div => format!(\"tl_div64({l}, {r})\"),\n1074:            other => unreachable!(\"{other} is not arithmetic\"),\n1078:            BinOp::Div => format!(\"tl_div({l}, {r})\"),\n1085:            other => unreachable!(\"{other} is not arithmetic\"),\n","metadata":{"output":"65:function tl_div(a, b) {\n78:const ARITH64_HELPER: &str = r#\"function tl_div64(a, b) {\n173:function tl_sum(v) {\n178:function tl_sum64(v) {\n211:        (used.arith, ARITH_HELPER),\n212:        (used.arith64, ARITH64_HELPER),\n505:    arith: bool,\n506:    arith64: bool,\n617:                used.sum |= *which == Builtin::Sum && tir::runtime_elem(&arg.ty) == Some(&Type::Int);\n619:                    *which == Builtin::Sum && tir::runtime_elem(&arg.ty) == Some(&Type::Int64);\n625:                    used.arith64 = true;\n627:                    used.arith = true;\n815:        Kind::Arith { op, lhs, rhs } => arith(&t.ty, *op, expr(enums, lhs), expr(enums, rhs)),\n871:            Builtin::Sum => {\n873:                    format!(\"tl_sum({})\", expr(enums, arg))\n875:                    format!(\"tl_sum64({})\", expr(enums, arg))\n1052:/// One arithmetic expression at the width the node's type names. BigInt operators are exact\n1055:fn arith(ty: &Type, op: BinOp, l: String, r: String) -> String {\n1058:        // arithmetic, with no wrap to spell. Division by zero is the IEEE answer, Infinity,\n1059:        // which is why there is no `tl_div` guard here unlike the integer widths.\n1065:            other => unreachable!(\"{other} is not arithmetic\"),\n1069:            BinOp::Div => format!(\"tl_div64({l}, {r})\"),\n1074:            other => unreachable!(\"{other} is not arithmetic\"),\n1078:            BinOp::Div => format!(\"tl_div({l}, {r})\"),\n1085:            other => unreachable!(\"{other} is not arithmetic\"),\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"grep -n \"tl_sum\\|tl_div\\|Sum\\|arith\" src/emit_js.rs | head -40","time":{"start":1788381796475,"end":1788381796477}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Let me check how the JS backend handles Float division by zero etc. and how `arith` is called. Also let me look at the emit_js.rs `show` handling again — it prints via `String({value})` for Float. For Lua, the equivalent of `String(value)` for a float needs to produce JS-identical bytes.\n\nLet me also check the comparison handling for floats — Lua's `<` on floats is fine. And `==` on floats: NaN ≠ NaN. JS `==`/`===` NaN ≠ NaN too. Good. Lua's comparisons on floats behave IEEE. So only the printer needs the hook.\n\nAlso `tostring` on integers is fine (the existing Int | Int64 | Bool row). For Float we need a separate hook. \n\nWait — Lua's `tostring` on a Float produces e.g. \"inf\", \"-inf\", \"nan\"? Lua's `tostring(math.huge)` → \"inf\"; `tostring(0/0)` (nan) → \"nan\" or \"-nan\"? In Lua 5.3, `tostring(0/0)` → \"nan\" or \"-nan\"? PUC Lua's `tostring` uses `%.14g` format; `%.14g` for NaN prints \"nan\" or \"-nan\". Lua 5.4: `tostring(0/0)` gives \"-nan\"? Hmm, I don't remember exactly. But regardless, doesn't match JS's \"NaN\"/\"Infinity\".\n\nSo we need a host hook. The cleanest: add a `tl_show` host function in `run_lua` (in src/lib.rs, right next to where `print` is injected), and have `show` in emit_lua.rs call it for `Type::Float`. But wait — the emitted Lua source references `tl_show`, which must be defined in the Lua runtime before the chunk runs. Since it's host-injected (set as a global), it will exist at runtime. But the step_*.rs snapshot tests just snapshot the emitted source without running it — that's fine, calls to undefined-at-snapshot-time functions like `print` already appear.\n\n.\n\nBut wait, should the hook be emitted in the Lua source (as a `*_HELPER` constant) or host-injected? The task says \"using Lua host-hook syntax\". I interpret \"host-hook\" as the `tl_*` convention — a function that the host provides. The `print` function is exactly that. So adding `tl_show` as a host function injected in `run_lua` fits. \n\nHmm, but there's a wrinkle: the `sum` helper also needs to handle floats? Let me look at how `sum` works for Float. In emit_lua.rs, `tl_sum(v, narrow)` starts `acc = 0`, adds each element. For a Float element, `acc + v[i]` — if acc starts as integer 0 and v[i] is a float, Lua promotes to float. Then the result type would be float if any element is float. But `narrow` param: `used.arith |= which == Builtin::Sum && runtime_elem(arg_ty) == Some(&Type::Int)`. For Float elements, narrow=false. The emitted call passes `runtime_elem(&arg.ty) == Some(&Type::Int)` → false for Float. So `tl_sum(v, false)`. `acc = 0` (integer), `acc = acc + v[i]` promotes to float. Result: float. Then printed. But what about the output type? The program's result type would be Float, so the printer uses the Float hook. Fine. But careful: `acc = 0` then `acc + v[i]` where v[i] is e.g. 0.5 →  ‎0.5. Good. For an empty Vec of Float... `sum` of empty Vec — does the checker allow that? For Int, `sum_empty.yaml` exists. For Float it'd be 0.0? The acc starts as integer 0, and empty gives integer 0 but the type is Float... Hmm, that would print \"0\" via the Float hook (0.0 as Float but value 0 → \"0\"). And JS's `tl_sum` for floats? Let me check the JS backend's sum for Float.\n\nLet me look at the JS backend's sum helper and arith helper in detail. Also check the JS printer for Float and whether they had to handle sum specially.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_063dc9865001EQVXjIjUxQXtr6","sessionID":"ses_f9c24d349ffeKHnaVF2lrb6ncE","messageID":"msg_063dc6baf001Z1ANZ05PPHe2Zy"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788381796480,"sessionID":"ses_f9c24d349ffeKHnaVF2lrb6ncE","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_3889455949ed4784b9741a98","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-lua/src/emit_js.rs","limit":60,"offset":1060},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-lua/src/emit_js.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1060:         match op {\n1061:             BinOp::Add => format!(\"({l} + {r})\"),\n1062:             BinOp::Sub => format!(\"({l} - {r})\"),\n1063:             BinOp::Mul => format!(\"({l} * {r})\"),\n1064:             BinOp::Div => format!(\"({l} / {r})\"),\n1065:             other => unreachable!(\"{other} is not arithmetic\"),\n1066:         }\n1067:     } else if *ty == Type::Int64 {\n1068:         match op {\n1069:             BinOp::Div => format!(\"tl_div64({l}, {r})\"),\n1070:             BinOp::Rem => format!(\"tl_rem64({l}, {r})\"),\n1071:             BinOp::Mul => format!(\"BigInt.asIntN(64, {l} * {r})\"),\n1072:             BinOp::Add => format!(\"BigInt.asIntN(64, {l} + {r})\"),\n1073:             BinOp::Sub => format!(\"BigInt.asIntN(64, {l} - {r})\"),\n1074:             other => unreachable!(\"{other} is not arithmetic\"),\n1075:         }\n1076:     } else {\n1077:         match op {\n1078:             BinOp::Div => format!(\"tl_div({l}, {r})\"),\n1079:             BinOp::Rem => format!(\"tl_rem({l}, {r})\"),\n1080:             // Math.imul is the only exact 32-bit multiply here: a plain `*` loses the low\n1081:             // bits once the true product passes 2^53.\n1082:             BinOp::Mul => format!(\"Math.imul({l}, {r})\"),\n1083:             BinOp::Add => format!(\"(({l} + {r}) | 0)\"),\n1084:             BinOp::Sub => format!(\"(({l} - {r}) | 0)\"),\n1085:             other => unreachable!(\"{other} is not arithmetic\"),\n1086:         }\n1087:     }\n1088: }\n1089: \n1090: fn js_op(op: BinOp) -> &'static str {\n1091:     match op {\n1092:         BinOp::Eq => \"===\",\n1093:         BinOp::Ne => \"!==\",\n1094:         BinOp::Lt => \"<\",\n1095:         BinOp::Le => \"<=\",\n1096:         BinOp::Gt => \">\",\n1097:         BinOp::Ge => \">=\",\n1098:         other => unreachable!(\"{other} is not a comparison\"),\n1099:     }\n1100: }\n1101: \n1102: fn js_string(s: &str) -> String {\n1103:     let mut out = String::with_capacity(s.len() + 2);\n1104:     out.push('\"');\n1105:     for c in s.chars() {\n1106:         match c {\n1107:             '\"' => out.push_str(\"\\\\\\\"\"),\n1108:             '\\\\' => out.push_str(\"\\\\\\\\\"),\n1109:             '\\n' => out.push_str(\"\\\\n\"),\n1110:             '\\r' => out.push_str(\"\\\\r\"),\n1111:             '\\t' => out.push_str(\"\\\\t\"),\n1112:             // U+2028 and U+2029 are line terminators in JavaScript but not in JSON, so a string\n1113:             // containing them would end the literal early if written through.\n1114:             c if (c as u32) < 0x20 || c == '\\u{7f}' || c == '\\u{2028}' || c == '\\u{2029}' => {\n1115:                 out.push_str(&format!(\"\\\\u{:04x}\", c as u32));\n1116:             }\n1117:             c => out.push(c),\n1118:         }\n1119:     }\n\n(Showing lines 1060-1119 of 1122. Use offset=1120 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"        match op {\n            BinOp::Add => format!(\"({l} + {r})\"),\n            BinOp::Sub => format!(\"({l} - {r})\"),\n            BinOp::Mul => format!(\"({l} * {r})\"),\n            BinOp::Div => format!(\"({l} / {r})\"),\n            other => unreachable!(\"{other} is not arithmetic\"),\n        }\n    } else if *ty == Type::Int64 {\n        match op {\n            BinOp::Div => format!(\"tl_div64({l}, {r})\"),\n            BinOp::Rem => format!(\"tl_rem64({l}, {r})\"),\n            BinOp::Mul => format!(\"BigInt.asIntN(64, {l} * {r})\"),\n            BinOp::Add => format!(\"BigInt.asIntN(64, {l} + {r})\"),